<title>UK: British Land, Compass set to join FTSE 100.</title>
<headline>British Land, Compass set to join FTSE 100.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1997-03-12</dateline>
<text>
<p>Property group British Land Plc and contract caterer Compass Group Plc are poised to join the elite group of top 100 British companies after the committee managing the blue chip index meets later on Wednesday.</p>
<p>The market capitalisation of those two companies should be sufficient to place them into the upper tier after the quarterly rejig of the FTSE 100 and London's other indicies.</p>
<p>At the close of business on Tuesday, British Land was ranked number 91 in terms of market capitalisation, valued at around 2.411 billion stg($3.8577 billion) with Compass valued at 2.2822 billion stg ($3.6515 billion), making it the 94th largest company.</p>
<p>Retailer Argos Plc and sugar group Tate &amp; Lyle Plc are in the front line of stocks most likely to be relegated to the FTSE 250 -- the second division of mid-sized UK listed stocks.</p>
<p>At Tuesday's London close, Tate ranked at 102, with a market cap of 1.9593 billion stg ($3.135 billion), with Argos valued at 1.9198 billion stg ($3.0717 billion), placing it at 106 in the league table.</p>
<p>Changes will formally take effect on March 24, said a spokeswoman for FTSE International -- a joint venture between the Financial Times and the London Stock Exchange which administers the UK stock indicies.</p>
<p>Traditionally, membership of the FTSE 100 is regarded as bestowing a higher international profile to individual stocks. its members are also likely to be bought by &quot;tracker&quot; funds which aim to simply match the performance of leading indicies.</p>
<p>British Land, which closed at 555-1/2p on Tuesday, only 3-1/2p below its 12-month high, was down 9p to 546-1/2p by 1415 GMT. Compass Group was up 2p to 718p versus a 12-month high of 745p.</p>
<p>Argos slipped 1-1/2p to 667-1/2p, well below its 12-month high of 800p, while Tate &amp; Lyle eased 1-1/2p to 443-1/2p compared with a 12-month best of 514-1/2p.</p>
<p>In the last quarterly adjustment of index members in December, fund manager Mercury Asset Management Plc and business services group Hays Plc were added to the FTSE 100.</p>
<p>They replaced chemicals group Courtaulds Plc and glassmaker Pilkington Plc.</p>
